{"_data": [{"type": "metadata", "contents": [{"content": "", "type": "lesson_name"}, {"content": "Other Popular Programming Languages", "type": "unit_name"}, {"content": 6, "type": "position"}, {"content": "stub", "type": "origin"}, {"content": 1, "type": "lesson"}, {"content": "B1.1", "type": "level"}]}, {"type": "achievment_test", "contents": [{"content": "false", "type": "test"}]}, {"type": "target_word_describer", "contents": [{"content": "These are words you use when talking about popular programming and scripting languages.", "type": "text"}]}, {"type": "objectives", "contents": [{"content": "Describe a general-purpose computer programming language in spoken and written situations.", "type": "primary"}, {"content": "Discusses a range of options for potential actions at work through the correct use of grammar, vocabulary; level of formality and purpose, and cohesion and coherence aspects appropriate to this level.  Exchanges simple information on principles, concepts, and features of general purpose computer programming languages.", "type": "secondary_1"}]}, {"content": "enpe7n5le01ob06re1img01.jpg", "type": "background_image"}, {"type": "learning_points", "contents": [{"content": "Present Perfect: Negative Form Versus Past Simple", "type": "heading"}, {"content": "We use the <strong>present perfect</strong> to talk about events that started in the past but are still in progress now. Additionally, it is also used to talk about past actions that have an effect on the present somehow.", "type": "describer"}, {"content": "The structure of the <strong>present perfect</strong> is the following:<br/><br/>  Subject + Have/has + verb (past participle) + complement<br/><br/> Examples:<br/>  <span class=\"italic\">I <strong>have</strong> only <strong>programmed</strong> in C++.</span><br/><br/>  To make the <strong>negative</strong> form of the present perfect tense, use <strong>not</strong> or its contraction <strong>n't</strong> after <strong>have</strong> or <strong>has</strong>, depending on the subject.<br/>  Check the following structure:<br/><br/>  Subject + Have/has + not + verb (past participle) + complement<br/><br/>  Examples: <br/> <span class=\"italic\">She <strong>has not used</strong> Python before.<br/> They <strong>haven't trained</strong> anyone to program in Perl.</span><br/><br/>  Note 1: You have to choose between <strong>have</strong> and <strong>has</strong> depending on the subject. If the subject is the third person singular, (he, she, it, or any proper noun that you can replace with any of these) you use <strong>has</strong>; for any other subject, you use <strong>have</strong>.<br/><br/>  Note 2: The past participle of the verb depends on the type of verb (regular or irregular). If we use regular verbs, we only use the <strong>-ed</strong> ending because the past and past participle form of these verbs are the same. Contrastively, if we use irregular verbs, we must use their proper form. Remember that you must learn the past participle form of irregular verbs by heart; you cannot only add the <strong>-ed</strong> ending to them.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "There are some tasks that I have not automatized yet. ", "type": "example"}, {"content": "There haven't ever been any cost for licensing or using them. ", "type": "example"}, {"content": "I've seen some other issues, but I haven't had much trouble handling them.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "<br><br><p><strong>Past Simple</strong></p> <p>We use the <strong>past simple</strong> to refer to events that started and ended in the past. The structure is the following:</p> <p style=\"text-align: center;\">&nbsp;Subject + <strong>verb in past form</strong> + complement</p> <p>&nbsp;Example:</p> <p><i>I <strong>finished</strong> checking the system components of the new language code.</i></p> <p><i>I <strong>taught</strong> the programmers how to code using Java yesterday.</i></p> <p>&nbsp;</p> <p>To make negative statements in the <strong>past simple,</strong> we use the auxiliary <strong>did not</strong>.</p> <p>This is the structure:</p> <p style=\"text-align: center;\">Subject + <strong>did not</strong> + verb (base form) + complement</p> <p>Example:</p> <p><i>I <strong>did not</strong> finish the configuration on time.</i></p> <p><i>The team <strong>did not</strong> have to transfer the information manually.</i></p> <p>&nbsp;</p> <p>To ask questions in the <strong>past simple,</strong> we use the auxiliary <strong>did</strong> at the beginning of the statement or right after the <strong>wh-</strong> question word.</p> <p style=\"text-align: center;\">&nbsp;(WH) + <strong>Did</strong> + subject +verb (base form) + complement</p> <p>Example:</p> <p><i>What time <strong>did</strong> you send the information to the programmers yesterday?</i></p> <p><i><strong>Did</strong> you find any problems with the software configuration?</i></p>", "type": "text"}]}, {"type": "learning_points", "contents": [{"content": "Tips for Writing a Paragraph", "type": "heading"}, {"content": "There are some tips you should follow when writing paragraphs to talk about specific information.", "type": "describer"}, {"content": "When you write a paragraph, you should follow certain pieces of advice that could help you make clear and straightforward pieces of writing.<br/> The first thing you must bear in mind is having a topic sentence. The topic sentence is the base that will endure the entire paragraph; it is the main idea that you will develop through the text. Then, you need some supporting ideas. These ideas help you build an accurate and organized definition of your topic. You also have to include reasons, examples, names, numbers, and figures. By using them, you will support the information that you are giving and ensure that your statements are substantiated.", "type": "text"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "trouble ", "type": "name"}, {"content": "difficulties; problems; inconveniences", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(noun)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: I've seen some other issues, but I haven't had much trouble handling them.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "enpe7n5le01ob06aud01", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "licensing ", "type": "name"}, {"content": "a permission given by the appropriate authority to use something", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(noun)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: There haven't ever been any costs for licensing or using them.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "enpe7n5le01ob06aud02", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "migrate ", "type": "name"}, {"content": "to carry information to a different environment ", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(verb)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: We use it for maintenance of existing applications but not to develop new ones; I suggest migrating the programs based on COBOL to a more modern platform.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "enpe7n5le01ob06aud03", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "compile ", "type": "name"}, {"content": "to put together", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(verb)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: As there is no need to compile these languages before running them, the process won't take long.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "enpe7n5le01ob06aud04", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "open source ", "type": "name"}, {"content": "software that can be used, accessed, and modified freely, without costs of licensing or purchase", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(noun)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: Actually, these languages are open source; they are available for free access and use. ", "type": "example"}, {"content": "enpe7n5le01ob06aud05", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "automatized", "type": "name"}, {"content": "the quality of being done by automatic means", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(adjective)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: There are some tasks that I have not automatized yet.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "enpe7n5le01ob06aud06", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "takeaway_extension", "contents": [{"content": "Make a comparative chart where you describe scripting languages Perl, Ruby, Python and other programming languages like Java and Cobol. ", "type": "text"}, {"content": "On the web, find more information about other scripting languages. ", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Highlight the advantages of them and count the ones with the highest number of items. ", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Write a text describing the best ones and present them to a partner.", "type": "text"}]}, {"type": "takeaway_assessment", "contents": [{"content": "I compare scripting languages and programming languages. ", "type": "text"}, {"content": "I find further information about programming languages. ", "type": "text"}, {"content": "I explain the advantages of the best ones.", "type": "text"}]}], "type": "sena", "labs": [{"exercises": [{"questions": [], "template_override": 1, "stimuli_ids": [0], "_data": [{"type": "instructions", "contents": [{"content": "I can understand written information about scripting languages like Perl, Ruby and Python.", "type": "Learning Outcomes"}, {"content": "I can read and analyze basic information on other programming languages like Java and COBOL.", "type": "Learning Outcomes"}, {"content": "I can write a text about information I recall on other popular programs. ", "type": "Learning Outcomes"}, {"content": "What other programming languages have you heard of different from C, C++, and C#?", "type": "Free Header Item"}, {"content": "What programming language would you use to automatize tasks? Why?", "type": "Free Header Item"}]}, {"type": "group", "contents": [{"content": "Welcome", "type": "group_name"}]}, {"content": {"incorrect_feed